      "strongest_counter_limit": "fxe4 removes a defender and must be checked before relying on cooperative Rxd5. Its objective strongest rank and best subsequent play require owned completed comparison; not established by this legal replay or a participant thought transcript.",
      "essential_condition": "Losing the cheap Pe6xd5 changes the cost of denial. Rc5xd5 remains legal and requires every legal recapture/forcing reply to be assessed. If fxe4 removes Pe4, the defender and realization must be updated: after Rdxe4 the d-file rook can defend Nd5.",
      "events_not_required_identically": "A useful White plan need not play Nd5 immediately or retain a pawn on e4 forever. Ne2/Ng3, Na2/Nc1/Nb3, queenside a4 and altered rook/knight activity can realize the retained flexibility.",
      "counterexamples": [
        "Remaining Rc5 does not make the lost pawn capture redundant; it gives up a rook if White can recapture. Conversely, a rook-for-knight offer can have compensation, so nominal cost alone does not prove ineffective defense.",
        "Red1 a5 Ne2 exf5 Ng3 is a printed useful route: a5 need not stop every White plan to be sound.",
        "The source explicitly gives Ne2 Rg8!? as a defense to Ng3. A fixed future Ng3 event is not a universal core condition.",
        "After fxe6 fxe6, Black gains structure, bishop scope and f6-f5. The later game win includes missed alternatives and is not proof of a forced root chain."
      ],
